legendre-transformation-0ab35037.symmetry_origin:
The Legendre transformation arises from the need to express physical quantities in terms of conjugate variables. In mechanics, the conjugate momentum 
(
p_i
) is defined through the variation of the Lagrangian 
(ℓ
) with respect to the generalized velocity 
(q_i
). By defining the Hamiltonian 
(
H
) using the Legendre transform, we obtain a set of equations of motion (Hamilton's equations) that have a more symmetric and often more insightful form, relating the time derivatives of coordinates and momenta.

legendre-transformation-0ab35037.conceptual_definition:
The Legendre transformation is a mathematical technique used in physics to change the set of independent variables of a function, often from generalized coordinates and velocities to generalized momenta and coordinates. In the context of mechanics, it is used to switch between the Lagrangian formulation, which is a function of generalized coordinates 
(
q_i
) and generalized velocities 
(
q_i
), and the Hamiltonian formulation, which is a function of generalized coordinates 
(
q_i
) and generalized momenta 
(
p_i
). The resulting function, the Hamiltonian 
(
H
), represents the total energy of a system in many conservative cases.
